          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so for the same n_iter/data_width it uses fewer FFs than fully pipelined (fewer pipeline registers) while still delivering 1 result/cycle — it can therefore beat pipelined on the luts_plus_ffs objective at equal accuracy. The throughput and batch-window constraints are met the same way as pipelined (1 result/cycle, latency ceil(N/m)+2 cycles must fit in 0.44 us). I sweep m across its full range because m is the key area lever: large m saves FFs but lengthens the combinational path per stage, which can cut Fmax and risk the 0.44 us batch bound, so the m-vs-Fmax trade-off is exactly where the Pareto front lives. Same n_iter/data_width/guard ranges as pipelined for a comparable accuracy/area slice."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is a 1 MHz tick issuing 32 requests that must all return within 0.44 us, with max_abs_err <= 2^-12 and a minimise-LUTs+FFs objective. The 32 Msps throughput bound and the 0.44 us batch window both push toward 1-result-per-cycle architectures, which is exactly what pipelined and pipelined_m provide (iterative/unrolled_k are not in my assigned set anyway). pipelined is the safe baseline that will almost certainly clear the system constraints, so I give it the larger share to map the accuracy-vs-area curve thoroughly. pipelined_m is the area-reduction variant (fewer pipeline registers at the same throughput), so it is where the cheapest points on the front are likely to come from; I give it a substantial share and sweep m fully because the m-vs-Fmax interaction is the real trade-off. I keep n_iter and data_width at their full registry ranges in both families because accuracy_bits (maximised) and max_abs_err (constrained) are set by n_iter/data_width while the area objective is set by data_width/n_iter — the entire Pareto front spans that 2D slice, and narrowing it prematurely would under-cover the hypervolume. angle_guard and frac_guard are swept across their full ranges since they are cheap knobs that trade a small amount of area against accuracy_bits. Rounding is left to both choices so the search can find whether round or trunc gives better accuracy_bits per LUT."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The exploration has successfully mapped the feasible Pareto front for the `pipelined` and `pipelined_m` families, which are the only architectures capable of meeting the strict system constraint `sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44` (iterative/unrolled_k would fail due to low throughput/latency). The current front covers a wide range of the objective space: `luts_plus_ffs` from 1763 to 4036 (spanning the HV reference of 4000) and `accuracy_bits` from 14.1 to 19.7 (well above the HV reference of 12). The best design (`pipelined_m` with `data_width=19`, `n_iter=17`, `m=2`) achieves a minimal area of 1763 LUTs+FFs while satisfying all constraints (throughput 164 MSPS >= 32, error 5.59e-05 <= 2^-12, and system latency well within bounds). Since this is the final round and the front is well-distributed across the trade-off curve, further exploration would yield diminishing returns. The selection rule (min `luts_plus_ffs`) will pick the 1763 design, which is robustly feasible."
,
